/bin/zsh -lc "task-board m 'set_status(STORY-260715-anxje6, status=analysis); set_details(STORY-260715-anxje6, title=\"Least-privilege continuous integration and release quality gates\", description=\"Establish credential-free pull-request validation and protected release orchestration for the generated Apple workspace, shared tunnel core, relay, repository specifications, security controls, versioning, and release evidence.\", scope=\"In scope: GitHub Actions permission and trust boundaries, immutable action pins, untrusted-fork behavior, generated-project and legacy SwiftPM checks, shared-core and protocol tests, credential-free Apple builds, relay release-matrix orchestration, board and specification validation, secret and dependency scanning, SBOM and notice gates, semantic version and release-note validation, protected GitHub Environments, serialized production jobs, artifact retention, and provenance metadata. Out of scope: issuing Apple credentials, implementing product features, defining relay protocol behavior, performing notarization or TestFlight uploads, and changing App Review or legal policy.\", ac=\"1. Pull-request workflows run from a clean checkout with minimum read permissions, cannot read production environments or secrets, and report signing-required work as unavailable rather than passed. 2. Required checks deterministically validate generated-project drift, the preserved SwiftPM product, shared-core and protocol suites, credential-free iOS and macOS targets, and declared relay targets. 3. Board/specification, semantic version/tag, release-note, secret, dependency, license, SBOM, and third-party-notice gates fail on controlled negative fixtures. 4. Production release workflows use protected GitHub Environments, explicit least-privilege permissions, immutable action revisions, masked credentials, and concurrency that serializes release publication. 5. Every retained artifact records source commit, dependency lockfiles, toolchain and SDK versions, relay manifest and protocol version, bundle versions, signing identity metadata without private material, checksums, and retention class. 6. A CI threat and failure-path verification proves forks, reruns, cancellations, cache poisoning attempts, missing credentials, and partial releases cannot bypass required gates or leak secrets.\"); set_status(STORY-260715-19mjyn, status=analysis); set_details(STORY-260715-19mjyn, title=\"Reproducible relay supply chain, SBOM, and compliance evidence\", description=\"Productize the approved relay toolchain into reproducible release assets, verified Apple-bundle inputs, dependency inventories, SBOMs, notices, vulnerability policy, checksums, provenance, and update or rollback evidence.\", scope=\"In scope: pinned relay and dependency revisions, pinned or recorded build containers and toolchains, Linux and macOS x86_64 and arm64 release builds, independent reproducibility checks, protocol and native or emulated smoke tests, file and build identities, SHA-256 manifests, deterministic Apple-bundle selection, SBOM generation, license and third-party notices for HEV, hev-task-system, hev-socks5-core, lwIP, the SSH engine, and relay dependencies, vulnerability and secret scanning, provenance attestations, release staging, and supply-chain rollback. Out of scope: changing protocol semantics, remote install implementation, Apple code signing, notarization, TestFlight, App Review submission, runtime traffic behavior, and silently accepting unpinned inputs or unexplained binary differences.\", ac=\"1. Clean pinned builds produce exactly one uniquely named relay asset for each declared OS and architecture and no undeclared network fetch or mutable dependency input. 2. Two isolated builds reproduce each unsigned asset bit-for-bit or fail with a documented, reviewed nondeterminism that is removed before release. 3. A generated manifest binds protocol version, platform, architecture, size, SHA-256, build identity, source revision, dependency lock digest, and toolchain identity; CI and Apple bundle verification reject every mismatch, omission, duplicate, or ambiguous selection. 4. Machine-readable SBOMs and human-readable notices cover every shipped relay, HEV, lwIP, SSH, and transitive dependency with license text, source revision, and distribution obligation. 5. Release assets pass protocol conformance, hostile-input, stdio boundary, native or approved emulated smoke, self-hash, vulnerability, secret, and notice-completeness gates. 6. The release evidence traces source to bundled bytes and records checksums, attestations, scanners, exceptions, update procedure, rollback procedure, and residual risk without storing credentials.\"); set_status(STORY-260715-c1qsc6, status=analysis); set_details(STORY-260715-c1qsc6, title=\"Signed, notarized, and recoverable macOS distribution\", description=\"Deliver a protected macOS release path for ReluxProxyMac and its packet-tunnel extension, including archive assembly, minimum entitlements, nested signing, hardened runtime, notarization, stapling, Gatekeeper validation, DMG publication, stable authenticated assets, migration checks, and rollback.\", scope=\"In scope: release signing contract, Developer ID credentials and least-privilege ownership, host and extension identifiers, provisioning and entitlements, relay and notice embedding, version propagation, release archive, inside-out nested signing, hardened runtime, signature inspection, notarization, stapling, Gatekeeper assessment, deterministic DMG assembly where signing permits, checksums and provenance, versioned and stable ReluxProxy.dmg assets on a private GitHub repository, authenticated retrieval, clean install, upgrade, legacy-product migration or coexistence, rollback, revocation, and release evidence. Out of scope: iOS signing, TestFlight, product feature implementation, changing the approved bundle or migration decision, weakening entitlements to satisfy signing, public unauthenticated private-repository assets, and storing certificates or keys in the repository or board.\", ac=\"1. The release archive contains the approved macOS host and exactly one embedded tunnel extension, bundled relay assets and notices, and exact bundle, App Group, Keychain, packet-tunnel, deployment-target, and version metadata. 2. Every nested executable is signed inside-out with the intended Developer ID identity, compatible profiles and minimum entitlements, hardened runtime, timestamps, and a passing strict signature assessment. 3. The distributed DMG is notarized, stapled, accepted by Gatekeeper on a clean supported Apple-silicon Mac, and accompanied by verified checksums, SBOM or notice links, source and relay provenance, and version metadata. 4. Private-repository publication exposes immutable versioned assets plus the stable ReluxProxy.dmg name through authenticated GitHub access, updates the stable asset without a partial window, and proves unauthenticated access is not assumed. 5. Clean install, upgrade from the supported legacy release, approved coexistence or migration, extension approval, connect or disconnect smoke, uninstall, previous-version rollback, bad-release withdrawal, and credential revocation are reproducible. 6. Production credentials are environment-scoped, masked, rotation-tested, absent from forks and retained artifacts, and release jobs are serialized with an auditable evidence bundle.\"); set_status(STORY-260715-243sh0, status=analysis); set_details(STORY-260715-243sh0, title=\"iOS archive, TestFlight, and App Store binary delivery\", description=\"Deliver a protected iOS distribution path for ReluxProxyIOS and its packet-tunnel extension, from release signing and archive inspection through TestFlight upload, named-device lifecycle validation, App Store validation, provenance, and build withdrawal or supersession.\", scope=\"In scope: Apple Distribution and App Store Connect credential ownership, distribution profiles, host and extension identifiers and entitlements, archive and export configuration, relay and compliance-resource embedding, semantic and build version propagation, embedded-profile and entitlement inspection, TestFlight upload and processing, internal tester rollout, physical iPhone installation and VPN lifecycle checks, privacy manifest and export-compliance validation, symbols, App Store binary preflight, source-to-archive provenance, signed-build reproducibility boundaries, and rollback or supersession runbooks. Out of scope: Developer ID and notarization, public App Store marketing or legal copy owned by the review story, product feature implementation, simulator-only acceptance, live production credentials in tests, and claiming byte-identical signed IPAs when Apple signing introduces nondeterminism.\", ac=\"1. A clean release archive embeds exactly the approved iOS tunnel extension and required relay or compliance resources, uses matching distribution profiles, and passes automated bundle ID, team, App Group, Keychain, packet-tunnel entitlement, version, and embedded-profile inspection. 2. Protected CI allocates a monotonic build number, exports the declared App Store method, uploads to App Store Connect, waits for processing, records identifiers, and distributes the same candidate to the intended TestFlight group without exposing credentials. 3. A named physical iPhone installs the TestFlight build and passes first launch, privacy and permission gates, extension start and stop, full or degraded capability, app termination, sleep or wake, network change, diagnostics, and uninstall acceptance. 4. The candidate passes App Store validation for architecture, symbols, privacy manifests, required-reason APIs where applicable, export compliance, bundle metadata, and prohibited private APIs. 5. Evidence binds source commit, dependency locks, generator and Xcode versions, relay manifest, protocol version, marketing and build versions, archive and export checksums, profiles, signing identities, and App Store Connect build ID while distinguishing reproducible unsigned inputs from signed outputs. 6. Expiring, withdrawing, superseding, or rejecting a TestFlight build and rotating or revoking credentials follows a tested runbook without corrupting version history or promoting an unverified binary.\"); set_status(STORY-260715-2dtdql, status=analysis); set_details(STORY-260715-2dtdql, title=\"Release operations, privacy and legal compliance, and App Review submission\", description=\"Create the operational control plane and evidence package that promotes verified relay, macOS, and iOS candidates, publishes truthful privacy and legal material, supports review with reproducible self-hosted SSH setup, submits the iOS app, and permits safe rollback or response.\", scope=\"In scope: release version manifest and go or no-go control, role separation and approvals, cross-platform release and rollback runbooks, current Apple guidance revalidation, public privacy policy and in-app parity, App Privacy labels, export-compliance and regional VPN licensing or storefront decisions, support and retention statements, App Review data-flow and entitlement-purpose evidence, degraded-mode and system-exclusion disclosure, user-owned SSH exit explanation, non-production review host or reproducible setup, review account or profile handling, metadata and screenshot evidence, release-candidate matrix, behavior-to-claim traceability, submission, response handling, incident and credential revocation, and durable audit evidence. Out of scope: inventing legal advice, changing product behavior to match unsupported claims, sharing production credentials, launching unsupported regions, implementing tunnel features, or bypassing negative Gate A0, P0, signing, TestFlight, privacy, or regression evidence.\", ac=\"1. A versioned release manifest and runbook identify candidate commits and artifacts, owners, approvals, required CI gates, promotion order, communication, retention, abort criteria, rollback, revocation, and post-release checks for relay, macOS, and iOS. 2. A currently dated primary-source review plus accountable product, privacy, legal, security, and regional approvals produce public policy, App Privacy labels, export-compliance answers, retention or deletion commitments, and storefront availability that match actual behavior. 3. The App Review package accurately explains the VPN data path, Network Extension purpose, user-owned SSH exit, observer boundaries, system exclusions, full and degraded modes, diagnostics, support data, and regional limits without claiming anonymity or a Relux-operated exit service. 4. Reviewers receive a reproducible test profile or isolated SSH host and instructions that exercise the submitted binary without exposing private production credentials; access is scoped, monitored for abuse, rotated, and removed after review. 5. The exact signed candidates pass the supported simulator, physical iPhone, Apple-silicon Mac, accessibility, privacy, leak, lifecycle, entitlement, notarization, TestFlight, artifact, migration, install, upgrade, and rollback matrix, with failures blocking promotion. 6. Submission metadata, screenshots, privacy and support URLs, binary and build identifiers, reviewer notes, communications, rejections, corrections, and resubmissions are preserved and traceable to the reviewed behavior and evidence revision.\")'" in /Users/iv/Developer/relux-proxy
